unit-postgresql-1.0.1 (1) keel; urgency=low

  * The conf script sets no password for any role. It opened with
    "set ${PGSQL_PASS:=postgres}" and gave the postgres role that value, so
    a build that declared nothing published a layer whose database
    superuser password was 'postgres', and a layer is fetched by name and
    reused: every appliance built on it carried the same known password,
    and one whose first boot hook failed kept it. Until now only
    keel-postgresql's conf.d/main took it away again, which every other
    recipe carrying this component would have had to remember. It is
    removed at the source. The cluster is created fresh, so the role has no
    password and authenticates nothing over TCP until
    firstboot.d/35pgsqlpass sets the real one from DB_PASS.

  * conf-vars is gone: PGSQL_PASS was the only variable it named and
    nothing reads it now.

  * tests/unit.bats fails when any conf script of the unit sets a database
    password at build time (SQL PASSWORD and IDENTIFIED BY, psql's
    \password, chpasswd, mysqladmin password, or a *_PASS variable), and
    tests/conf.bats runs the script with PGSQL_PASS in its environment and
    proves no role receives it.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Wed, 07 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000

unit-postgresql-1.0.0 (1) keel; urgency=low

  * The PostgreSQL component, extracted from the shared tree as a fab unit
    (decision 0010): the overlay of overlays/pgsql and the conf script of
    conf/pgsql, consumed through unit.d by every recipe that carries a
    PostgreSQL server. The package names, which the shared tree left in each
    recipe because it had no plans/turnkey/pgsql, are the component's plan
    now.

  * The cluster is told to listen on '::1,127.0.0.1', two literal addresses,
    which keel-postgresql's conf.d/main did until today. It moves here
    because binding is what whoever installs the server decides, and LAPP
    will carry this component without carrying that recipe. Debian's default,
    listen_addresses = 'localhost', binds the IPv4 loopback only: /etc/hosts
    maps ::1 to ip6-localhost and ip6-loopback and never to localhost, so an
    appliance reaching its own database over IPv6 found nothing listening.
    The conf script checks the file after writing it, and keel-postgresql
    keeps its own check on the built image, so the component is verified by
    the recipe rather than trusted.

  * PGSQL_PASS is named in conf-vars, the slot fab gained tonight for exactly
    this: a unit whose conf script reads a build time variable. Without it
    the variable would be dropped from the chroot environment, because
    _CONF_VARS is settled before any unit is looked at.

  * Two directory roots in the conf script, PG_LIB_DIR and PG_CONF_ROOT, are
    overridable defaults so the script can be run against scratch directories
    and measured. A build sets neither. The script does not ship: fab copies
    it into the chroot, runs it and removes it.

 -- Keel Linux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Sun, 27 Sep 2026 11:00:00 +0000
